FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/parser.rs:965 — `at_argument_label` treats any token except `RightParen`/`Comma`/`None` as "can begin an expression", contradicting its own doc at src/parser.rs:952 and `is_expression_start` at src/parser.rs:1702 — a variable `by` followed by an operator (`say f(by + 1)`, `say f(by is 1)`) is misread as a label, the `by` is dropped, and a previously valid program becomes a parse error — fix by testing the lookahead token against the `is_expression_start` set instead of a two-token blacklist.
2. [MAJOR] src/parser.rs:2178 — the `by`-label `advance()` sits in the generic `parse_postfix` argument loop, so every call (`pow(2, by 10)`, any user `to`) silently accepts a `by` label, while SPEC.md:1017-1018 defines `by` only for `text.split`/`text.join` (plus the loop step) — no test pins accept-or-refuse for non-split/join calls — fix by restricting the label to the documented builtins or pinning the generic rule with tests on both sides.
3. [MAJOR] src/parser.rs:961 — the lookahead skips `Newline` to decide `by` is a label, but nothing skips it after the `advance()` before `parse_expression()`, so `split("a,b", by\n",")` is classified as labelled yet parsed from a newline — neither side is tested — fix by adding a multiline-label test or by not skipping newlines in the lookahead.
4. [MINOR] tests/text_split_join_test.rs:13 — file header claims dotted `text.split` without `import text` is refused as `Unknown variable 'text'`, contradicting the pinned rule and test at tests/text_split_join_test.rs:625 (`text_split_reaches_dotted_without_an_import_and_that_is_the_rule`) — fix by correcting the stale comment to state the import is optional.
